      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(shell): resolve pwsh.exe/.cmd/.bat on PATH for Windows shell discovery",
        "body": "## Summary\n\nOn Windows, PowerShell 7 delivered via the Microsoft Store / App Execution Alias (and many portable installs) ships as pwsh.exe (also pwsh.cmd). \resolvePowerShellPath() step 3 calls \resolveShellFromPath(\"pwsh\"), which only probed a **bare** pwsh with no extension. \fs.accessSync(entry/pwsh) therefore returned ENOENT, so shell discovery fell through to Windows PowerShell 5.1 instead of using pwsh 7.\n\nThis is the actual Windows pwsh resolution gap (distinct from the custom-shell -c args discussion in #104053).\n\n## Root cause\n\n\resolveShellFromPath joined the entry with the bare name and never tried executable extensions on Windows:\n\n`\ts\nconst candidate = path.join(entry, name); // only \"pwsh\", never \"pwsh.exe\"\n`\n\n## Fix\n\nOn win32, also probe the usual executable extensions (.exe, .cmd, .bat), mirroring the existing pattern already used by \resolveWindowsBashPath (\"bash.exe\" / \"bash\"). Non-Windows behavior is unchanged.\n\n`\ts\nconst executableExtensions = process.platform === \"win32\" ? [\".exe\", \".cmd\", \".bat\"] : [];\nconst candidates = [base, ...executableExtensions.map((ext) => base + ext)];\n`\n\nThis makes a Store/portable pwsh.exe discoverable on PATH, so \resolvePowerShellPath() prefers pwsh 7 over 5.1.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- Added Windows-only tests in shell-utils.test.ts (guarded by isWin so Linux CI is unaffected):\n  - finds pwsh.exe when a bare pwsh is absent\n  - prefers a bare pwsh match over pwsh.exe when both exist\n  - \resolvePowerShellPath() resolves pwsh.exe on PATH instead of 5.1 when standard install dirs are empty\n- Existing tests unchanged; non-Windows \resolveShellFromPath behavior preserved.\r\n",
        "branch": "fix/windows-pwsh-path-discovery",
        "head_sha": "35666f641de84e9ff67c102bd3eba00c0dd73790",
